---

<PageLayout
  title="Conditions the melanocortin system treats — sorted by how good the evidence actually is"
  seoTitle="Melanocortin conditions: what's approved, what's in trials"
  kicker="The conditions map"
  description="One peptide family, five receptors, and a surprisingly long list of diseases. This is every condition a melanocortin drug treats or is being tested in, sorted into four honest tiers: approved, late-stage, mid-stage, and biology that explains a disease without treating it."
  seoDescription="Every condition a melanocortin drug treats or is being tested in, in four evidence tiers: approved, late-stage, mid-stage, and mechanism only."
  thread="The same signal, read in the skin, the brain, the adrenal and the gut — which is why the list of diseases looks random until you see the receptors underneath it."
  updated="2026"
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin'] },
    { name: 'Melanocortin receptor', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanocortin_receptor'] },
    { name: 'Proopiomelanocortin', type: 'MedicalEntity', sameAs: ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proopiomelanocortin'] },
  ]}
>
  <KeyTakeaways
    items={[
      `This page tracks ${CONDITIONS.length} conditions across four tiers: ${count('approved')} approved, ${count('late')} late-stage, ${count('mid')} mid-stage and ${count('mechanism')} where the biology explains the disease without treating it.`,
      'Every modern approval is for a rare condition with a clearly identified break in the pathway — a missing signal, a missing pigment response — not for a common disease.',
      'The list looks scattered because the receptors are: MC1R in skin and immune cells, MC2R in the adrenal, MC3R and MC4R in the brain, MC5R in glands.',
      'The mid-stage tier is where the hype lives. Several programmes there have already had a missed endpoint or a very small trial, and each row says so.',
      'A drug approved for one condition is not evidence for the next one. Tiers are assigned per condition, not per drug.',
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    If you came to melanocortins through one door — a tanning peptide, a weight-loss drug, a
    rare-disease diagnosis — the full list of conditions is a shock. Skin that burns in visible
    light. A child who never feels full. An adrenal gland that will not stop. An inflamed
    colon. It reads like four unrelated specialties, and it is one signalling system: a single
    precursor, <a href="/pomc">POMC</a>, cut into a handful of peptides and read by{' '}
    <a href="/receptors">five receptors</a> in five different places.
  </p>
  <p>
    That breadth is exactly why this field attracts loose claims. So the list below is sorted
    by the only thing that should sort it: how far the evidence has actually got. Approved
    means a regulator signed off.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={2} /><Cite n={3} /><Cite n={4} /> Everything
    below that line is a bet, and the further down you read, the longer the odds. Each row
    links to the page that carries its own citations.
  </p>

  {
    TIERS.map((t) => (
      <section>
        <h2 id={t.id} class="scroll-mt-24">{t.title}</h2>
        <p>{t.blurb}</p>
        <div class="not-prose mt-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
          {CONDITIONS.filter((c) => c.tier === t.id).map((c) => (
            <a
              href={c.href}
              class="plate group block p-4 no-underline"
            >
              <span class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
                <span class="font-medium text-[var(--text)] group-hover:text-[var(--accent)]">{c.name}</span>
                <span class="rounded-full bg-[var(--accent-soft)] px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-[var(--accent-strong)]">
                  {c.receptor}
                </span>
              </span>
              <span class="mt-1 block text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--text)]">{c.approach}</span>
              <span class="mt-1 block text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--text-muted)]">{c.status}</span>
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="dropped">Tried and dropped</h2>
  <p>
    A field that only lists its wins is a brochure. These programmes reached patients and were
    stopped, and each one taught the field something the survivors are built around — above
    all that pushing MC4R hard enough to matter can also push blood pressure.<Cite n={9} />
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      DROPPED.map((d) => (
        <li>
          <strong><a href={d.href}>{d.name}</a></strong> ({d.what.toLowerCase()}). {d.why}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>How to read the pattern</h2>
  <p>
    Look down the receptor column and the list stops looking random. The{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> rows are all about energy balance, run in both
    directions: agonists where the satiety signal is missing, antagonists where it is jammed
    on.<Cite n={8} /> The <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> rows split in two — pigment
    for the light-sensitivity diseases, where an oral agonist is now under regulatory
    review<Cite n={5} /> and a vitiligo trial is fully enrolled,<Cite n={6} /> and the receptor's quieter anti-inflammatory job for
    the gut, the eye and scarring skin, the story told on the{' '}
    <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a> page. The <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>{' '}
    rows are the newest idea on the board: not adding a signal but blocking one, at the adrenal,
    in diseases of too much ACTH.<Cite n={7} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Notice also where the recent approvals sit. The modern ones are rare, and in each the
    broken step is known: a missing precursor, a receptor that cannot reach its cilium, a
    pigment response that light itself cannot safely trigger. The ACTH-gel labels for
    sarcoidosis, MS relapse and infantile spasms are older and broader, and their evidence
    is thinner. That is the real lesson of the last few years. Melanocortin
    drugs have worked best as precise repairs to a defined fault, and least well as broad
    levers on common disease. For the drug-by-drug view of the same landscape, see the{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a>; for the two directions a receptor can be pushed, the{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-agonists">agonists</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-antagonists">antagonists</a> hubs.
  </p>

  <div class="aside-note not-prose">
    <p class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">Education, not a protocol</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text)]">
      This page maps where the evidence stands. It is not medical advice and gives no dosing,
      sourcing or treatment guidance. An investigational use is exactly that: unproven. See
      the <a href="/about">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <FAQ items={faqs} />

  <References items={references} />

  <RelatedPages slug="conditions" />
</PageLayout>
